WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.966
For the last 48 years.

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:11.177
Dr. Joyce Poole has been eavesdropping

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:16.266
on elephant families,

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:21.730
learning their language.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.358
I speak to elephants.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:28.737
I rumbled to them if they seem upset.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.239
I say hello and things.

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:36.995
Their vocabulary is very large.

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:40.665
Elephants have over 30 vocalizations.

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:44.377
Their rumbles the low frequency calls.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.839
Their roars, their trumpet,

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:51.217
their cries.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.724
Wants to suckle.

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:00.185
Yeah. Oh.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.270
So that's when it escalates.

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:05.940
That's when the suckle rumble didn't work.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:07.776
And then you have to escalate.

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:13.573
So saying, Mum, put your leg forward.

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:19.287
Mom is not being very co-operative.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.378
There's an ally, mother.

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:29.839
Just like a baby sitter.

00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:32.300
She doesn't have any milk, but

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.386
she can comfort the baby.

00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:38.681
Females from the age about three,
four years old

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:43.019
will try and take care of babies.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:50.151
That was called a Bruv Rumble.

00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:52.487
It’s a kind of (roar)

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.868
like, oh, poor me.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:01.871
And the ally mother responded with a reassuring

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:03.039
cou rumble

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:08.128
Okay,

00:02:09.212 --> 00:02:13.216
that's lovely.

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:18.012
See how reaching back there
she's trying to show the baby

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.807
where to suckle.

00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:26.354
So even though the ally mother

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:29.399
is not able to produce milk like mum does,

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:31.943
she does provide comfort

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:36.406
and that forms a close bond.

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:45.915
Still making that little sound that's like
I want it's a it's a sucker rumble.

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:48.209
But it's a more complaining one.

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:52.297
Obviously, it's not getting any milk.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:02:58.636
Okay.

00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:03.224
Now it's going to mum
to get some real milk

00:03:10.398 --> 00:03:11.691
suckle rumble

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:18.406
that she puts her leg forward
to let the calf suckle.

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:22.327
Very cute.

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:28.124
After decades

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:33.004
decoding the calls of savanna
elephants, Joyce has discovered a language

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:37.634
more elaborate than we ever imagined.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:47.018
Elephants
do combine different types of calls.

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:50.563
They can combine and make a rumble,
roar, rumble,

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:54.275
roar, rumble or a rumble trumpet.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:58.321
A snort trumpet.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.785
What we don't know is whether by combining

00:04:04.535 --> 00:04:07.163
those two different types,

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:11.209
they're making it, in a sense, a new word.

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:15.672
Elephant conversations
can be incredibly complex.

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:20.843
All of their rumbles
contain infrasonic components

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:25.598
that is below the level of human hearing.

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:30.645
And elephants can hear
these powerful rumbles from miles away

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:33.606
and also pick them up

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.609
as vibrations
through their feet and trunk.

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:40.488
Sometimes they can back and forth

00:04:40.488 --> 00:04:44.158
for up to an hour,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.411
literally having a kind of conversation

00:04:46.744 --> 00:04:49.455
or having a discussion about something.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.708
It's very obvious.

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:54.627
It's not just noise.

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.213
It has meaning

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:01.509
they’re So like us,

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:05.430
they communicate
about some of the same things that we do.

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:09.183
And this communication is part of the glue

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:13.688
that holds the whole family together.

